/**
 * @mainpage AMU Library
 *
 * @section intro_sec Introduction
 *
 * The AMU (Aerospace Measurement Unit) Library is a comprehensive, Arduino-compatible
 * C/C++ interface for communicating with AMU hardware over I2C and USB. It pairs a
 * high-level C++ class with full SCPI (Standard Commands for Programmable Instruments)
 * support for precise measurement and control of solar-cell test hardware.
 *
 * @subsection features_subsec Key Features
 *
 * - **Dual interface** — identical functionality over I2C (binary) and standards-compliant USB/SCPI (text)
 * - **Arduino compatible** — drops into Arduino and PlatformIO projects
 * - **Comprehensive measurements** — voltage, current, temperature, and sun-sensor data
 * - **I-V sweeps** — solar-cell characterization
 * - **Calibration** — built-in routines with non-volatile storage
 * - **Auxiliary control** — DAC output, heater control, and LED patterns
 *
 * @subsection getting_started_subsec Getting Started
 *
 * **Installation**
 *
 * 1. Download the library from the repository.
 * 2. Arduino IDE — install it as a ZIP library.
 * 3. PlatformIO — add it to `lib_deps` in `platformio.ini`.
 *
 * **Basic usage**
 *
 * @code
 * #include <Wire.h>
 * #include <amulib.h>
 *
 * // 0x00-0x07 are reserved by the I2C spec
 * #define AMU_SCAN_START_ADDRESS 0x08
 * #define AMU_SCAN_END_ADDRESS   0x20
 *
 * AMU amu;
 *
 * void setup() {
 *     Serial.begin(115200);
 *     Wire.begin();
 *     Wire.setClock(400000);
 *
 *     while (!Serial);
 *
 *     // The AMU constructor already registered the Wire transfer callback, so a
 *     // library-side scan can drive the bus as soon as Wire.begin() has run.
 *     if (amu_scan_for_devices(AMU_SCAN_START_ADDRESS, AMU_SCAN_END_ADDRESS) > 0) {
 *         Serial.println("AMU found! Initializing...");
 *
 *         amu.begin(amu_get_device_address(0));
 *
 *         // Set LED pattern to indicate device is ready
 *         amu.setLEDmode(AMU_LED_PATTERN_GREEN_FLASH);
 *
 *         Serial.println("AMU device ready!");
 *     } else {
 *         Serial.println("No AMU device found.");
 *     }
 * }
 *
 * void loop() {
 *     // Trigger a Voc measurement
 *     amu.triggerVoc();
 *
 *     // Wait for measurement to complete
 *     amu.waitUntilReady(250);
 *
 *     // Read measurement data
 *     amu_meas_t measurement = amu.readMeasurement();
 *
 *     Serial.print("Voltage: ");
 *     Serial.print(measurement.measurement, 6);
 *     Serial.print(" V\tTemperature: ");
 *     Serial.print(measurement.temperature, 2);
 *     Serial.println(" °C");
 *
 *     delay(1000);
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection api_ref_subsec C++ API Reference
 *
 * The C++ API exposes the device through a single @ref AMU "AMU" class plus a set of
 * structured data types (sun-sensor measurements, I-V sweep configurations, calibration
 * coefficients, and more). Most methods return a status code — check it and handle errors.
 * See the @ref AMU "class reference" for the full method list.
 *
 * @subsection command_overview_subsec Commands Overview
 *
 * The same functionality is reachable two ways: binary register access over I2C
 * (via the C++ `AMU` class), or text SCPI commands over USB. Over 100 SCPI
 * commands are organized into functional groups:
 *
 * - **System** — device control, status, identification, LED, and timestamps
 * - **Extended** — Bluetooth control and firmware updates (AMU3 ESP32 only)
 * - **DUT** — device-under-test configuration and metadata
 * - **Measurement** — channel reads: voltage, current, temperature, sun-sensor angles, internal voltages, pressure
 * - **ADC Channel** — per-channel ADC configuration and calibration
 * - **Execution & Calibration** — measurement triggers and calibration procedures
 * - **Sweep** — I-V sweep control, data, and metadata (Voc, Isc, solar-cell characterization)
 * - **Auxiliary** — DAC output, heater, and sun-sensor control
 * - **Memory** — Non-volatile memory calibration storage (USB only)
 *
 * Not every command applies to every AMU generation. The <a href="topics.html"><b>Commands</b></a>
 * tab has a hardware selector at the top of the page — set it to your device to filter the reference down to what that generation actually
 * supports.
 *
 * See the <a href="topics.html"><b>Commands</b></a> tab for the full per-command reference.
 *
 * @subsection examples_subsec Code Examples
 *
 * Complete, working sketches are in the `examples/` directory:
 *
 * - **simple** — discover the device on the I2C bus, read its notes, set an LED pattern, and trigger a Voc measurement
 * - **iv_sweep** — run a full I-V sweep with Voc/Isc
 * - **twi_passthrough** — USB/SCPI-to-I2C bridge that forwards host commands to one or more AMUs
 *
 * @subsection author_subsec Author
 *
 * Colin Mann - The Aerospace Corporation
 * Kerry Tang - The Aerospace Corporation
 */
